Cell["\<\
Figure 10.2  Cross-species scorecard pulling together the headline numbers \
from the preceding sections. Read it as a comparison of population-level \
summaries, not individual physiology; the per-section caveats (centroid \
\[NotEqual] individual; energetics first-order; magnetic landscape, not \
mechanism) all still apply.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{70, 70}, {2, 14}},
 FontSize->11,
 FontSlant->Italic,
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.35],ExpressionUUID->"b8aef3fb-1a70-4772-8709-b0eb5fc30e90"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["11.  New ideas, and how to test them", "Section",
 FontFamily->"Roboto Condensed",
 FontSize->30,
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.153, 0.51, 0.64],ExpressionUUID->"dc52d9a4-84e8-4f84-8331-04a257c2935a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Beyond reconstructing known journeys, this post surfaced two ",
 StyleBox["testable",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " ideas. Neither is settled here \[LongDash] both are hypotheses that our \
occurrence-centroid data can frame but not decide \[LongDash] and saying so \
plainly is the point."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"0129243d-b609-4e27-b210-d66360c6ab48"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Idea 1 \[LongDash] magnetic inclination as a latitude gauge.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " We showed (\[Section]8) that along all three flyways magnetic dip is \
almost a pure function of latitude (corr \[TildeTilde] ",
 "0.99",
 "), so the field ",
 StyleBox["could",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " in principle tell a bird how far north or south it is. What we could ",
 StyleBox["not",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " do is test whether the birds use it: occurrence centroids show the \
magnetic landscape traversed, not the cue employed. ",
 StyleBox["What would settle it:",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " individual tracking (Movebank archival tags, light-level geolocators) \
combined with magnetic-displacement experiments, not where-the-crowd-is \
data."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"f29a046e-53ee-4f52-96ca-5a47ebbe03fc"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Idea 2 \[LongDash] migration speed paced by photoperiod.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " We tested whether centroid speed rises when day length changes fastest (\
\[Section]6). The signal is weak and, under a cycle-respecting permutation \
null, ",
 StyleBox["not significant",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (best case red knot, r = ",
 "0.71",
 ", p = ",
 "0.08",
 ", n = 12). ",
 StyleBox["What would settle it:",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " a much longer time series \[LongDash] eBird Status & Trends weekly \
relative abundance gives 52 effort-corrected steps per year rather than our \
12, and individual tracks give departure dates that can be regressed directly \
on local photoperiod."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"f47f3d25-4971-4755-8e3f-12cbff15b7e0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Limitations, in one place.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Three caveats run through everything above. ",
 StyleBox["(1) Centroid \[NotEqual] individual:",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " a population centre of mass moves more slowly and more shortly than any \
bird in it (opposing motions cancel), so all speeds and distances here are \
lower bounds on the individual journey. ",
 StyleBox["(2) Effort bias:",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " presence weighting on a coarse grid blunts but does not erase the \
over-representation of densely-birded regions; southern and oceanic extremes \
are pulled toward where the observers are. ",
 StyleBox["(3) Sample, not census:",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " these are occurrence records, not a complete count, and the magnetic and \
energetic layers are model overlays, not measurements of the birds themselves."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"e80bcaa9-100a-4551-bcbf-20ccbfcd16f5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Test it yourself.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Every figure and number above regenerates from the public repository with \
four commands:"
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"306741a3-655b-469b-b339-a55356da7602"],

Cell["\<\
wolframscript -file wolfram/run_all.wls          # centroids, maps, \
animations
wolframscript -file wolfram/geomag.wls           # magnetic landscape (\
\[Section]8)
wolframscript -file wolfram/journey_metrics.wls  # speeds + photoperiod test \
(\[Section]6)
wolframscript -file wolfram/energetics.wls       # Pennycuick flight energy (\
\[Section]7)\
\>", "Program",
 CellMargins->{{60, 30}, {6, 6}},
 ShowStringCharacters->False,
 FontFamily->"Courier",
 FontSize->11,
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.95, 0.96, 0.98],ExpressionUUID->"099cf392-d23d-4a44-ae63-d730fa90f1a0"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["12.  Conclusions", "Section",
 FontFamily->"Roboto Condensed",
 FontSize->30,
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.153, 0.51, 0.64],ExpressionUUID->"e4b182d0-4805-45ef-8ad2-828ec6bb1975"],

Cell["\<\
With open data and a careful method, you can reconstruct continental-scale \
bird migration from a laptop. Hundreds of millions of volunteer observations, \
republished under an open licence with a citable DOI, plus a few dozen lines \
of Wolfram Language, are enough to recover the annual journeys of three of \
the planet's great migrants and to cross-validate them against a professional \
abundance model.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"d320eeda-9be2-4b22-ad8c-928bcc924f51"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "But the central lesson is a cautionary one. ",
 StyleBox["Naive pooling misleads.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " A single global centroid placed the red knot in the empty mid-Atlantic, \
~4,670 km/month from any real bird. Two disciplines rescue the analysis: ",
 StyleBox["bias-awareness",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (presence weighting on a coarse grid, so birders are not mistaken for \
birds) and ",
 StyleBox["per-flyway analysis",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (so disjoint populations are never averaged together), all on a ",
 StyleBox["proper spherical geometry",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (so longitudes are never averaged arithmetically). Get those three right \
and citizen-science occurrence data yield a faithful, validated picture of \
where the birds actually are."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"24175778-3f6e-400a-aa1e-efce19d951f9"]
}, Open  ]],

